<p style="color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem;">
By <a href="/en/urban-gavelin/" rel="author">Urban Gavelin</a>, <time datetime="2026-09-29">September 29, 2026</time>, Sales Strategy &amp; AI in B2B
</p>
<h1>The Technology Has Changed. The Craft Remains.</h1>
<img src={coverImage} alt="The Technology Has Changed. The Craft Remains." width="1280" height="720" class="hero-img" />
<p class="lead"><strong>When I started selling, deals were built through personal visits, trade shows, representation, and customer contact that stretched over years. Nobody had a database of decision makers. You knew them, or you knew someone who did.</strong></p>

<div class="section">
<h2>What Has Actually Changed</h2>
<p>The cost of reaching someone has gone to near zero. The cost of being worth listening to has not moved at all. When outreach was expensive, the contact itself was a signal. Someone had traveled, booked a meeting, prepared. That carried you a long way.</p><p>Now contact costs nothing, so it signals nothing. What remains is whether you have something to say that actually matters to the recipient. That is the same requirement it always was. The customer still needs to feel you are relevant, credible, and worth talking to. The difference is that you no longer get a discount on that requirement just for showing up.</p><p>Old-school relationship building and modern database-driven prospecting are not opposites, then. They are two versions of the same principle, and one can learn from the other.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>What AI Actually Changes</h2>
<p>AI does two things in sales, and only one of them gets talked about. The first is volume: more contacts, faster, cheaper. That part is real, and it is also the reason inboxes are dead.</p><p>The second is preparation, and it is underrated. Understanding a company's goals, reading their own communication, and connecting it to a problem you can genuinely solve used to be half a day of work per account. Now it takes twenty minutes.</p><p>That means the old kind of craft can suddenly be done at scale. Not the mass send. The preparation. That is where I put my clients' time.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>What You Do on Monday</h2>
<p>Take the five most important companies in your pipeline. Write one sentence per company about what they themselves say they are prioritizing this year. Not what you assume, what they actually state in their annual report, on their website, or in what leadership publishes. Then write one sentence about what is likely becoming difficult for them because of that priority.</p><p>If you have those two sentences, you have an opening that does not sound like everyone else's. If you do not have them, you do not have a deal, you have a hope.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>A Question to Carry With You</h2>
<p>How many of your active deals would survive the customer asking what you actually know about us?</p><p>That question is a useful filter. If the answer is uncomfortable, the gap is not in your tools, it is in the preparation behind them.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
<h3 itemprop="name">Has AI replaced the craft of selling?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<p itemprop="text">No, it has removed the advantage you once got simply for reaching a prospect. The craft of being relevant and credible is unchanged, but AI lets you prepare that relevance faster and at greater scale than before.</p>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
<h3 itemprop="name">What should sales teams actually use AI for?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<p itemprop="text">The highest-value use is preparation, not volume. Understanding a company's stated priorities and connecting them to a real problem you can solve turns a generic outreach into one that sounds different from everyone else's.</p>
</div>
</div>

